///! Marker traits.
///!
///! Traits with no methods. Implementing one doesn't add behavior; it
///! tells the compiler and other generic code that the type has certain
///! semantics.
///!
///! Enforcement status (v1.0):
///!   - `Copy` is enforced by the move-checker (a `Copy` type really is
///!     duplicated bitwise; assignment does not move it out).
///!   - `Send` / `Sync` are computed structurally and ARE checked at any
///!     explicit `where T: Send` / `where T: Sync` bound. However, the
///!     concurrency entry points (`thread::spawn`, the lock
///!     constructors) do NOT carry such bounds in v1.0, so they do not
///!     statically reject a non-`Send` payload (see `thread::spawn` for
///!     why — the scoped-thread idiom shares state via raw pointers).
///!     Treat `Send`/`Sync` as advisory at those boundaries for now.

namespace std::core::marker;

/// Types whose values can be duplicated by a bitwise copy. Integers,
/// floats, pointers, and pure-POD structs qualify; anything that owns
/// resources (heap storage, file handles, refcount) does not.
///
/// A `Copy` type never needs an explicit `clone` call; assignment and
/// argument passing duplicate it implicitly. `Copy` is therefore a
/// stronger promise than `Clone`.
type trait Copy { }

/// Types that are safe to transfer between threads. An owning value of
/// a `Send` type may cross a thread boundary without violating memory
/// safety.
type trait Send { }

/// Types that are safe to share between threads by shared reference.
/// `T: Sync` iff `&T: Send`.
type trait Sync { }
